(a) By agreement of the county and the city, this chapter shall be enforced by the county marshal's office. Nothing in this chapter shall be construed to prohibit the assistance of law enforcement persons within their jurisdiction. This chapter provides that the city shall be subject to the fees charged by the marshal's office and that all fees charged by the county marshal's office shall be retained by the county to defray the costs of the marshal's office related to animal control.
(b) Any person violating this chapter may be issued a citation and summons to appear before a court of competent jurisdiction, and the offending animal may be immediately impounded by the marshal's office pending disposition of the citation by the court. Any person violating this chapter shall be punished as provided in section 1-8.
(c) In addition to any other penalties allowed by law, the court, as part of any sentence, may do a combination of any of the following:
(1) Prohibit the offender from owning, possessing, or having on the offender's premises in the city any animal during the term of the sentence;
(2) Order that the animal be removed from the city;
(3) Order that the animal be surrendered to the county animal control facility;
(4) Order that the animal be humanely euthanized;
(5) Order restitution to the victim.
(Ord. No. 2007-10, § 10-27)

199:
(a) A public nuisance animal shall mean and include any animal that:
(1) Damages the property of anyone other than the owner;
(2) Attacks a human or another animal without provocation;
(3) Makes any vocalizations for an extended period of time. These time limits do not apply if the vocalizations are given as a warning to the presence of an intruder. Officers enforcing this subsection are not required to measure the vocalizations with the use of a sound level meter. Upon notification of a complaint concerning such vocalizations, the owner of such animal shall be given a written warning indicating that such animal is creating a disturbance, so long as the complainant provides the officer with the address of the owner. If the disturbance is not resolved within two days or a subsequent complaint is made for such animal by a different individual, who resides at a different location from the first complainant, a court summons shall be issued in accordance with the requirements of this chapter;
(4) Creates unsanitary conditions or offensive and objectionable odors in enclosures or surroundings and thereby creates unreasonable annoyance or discomfort to neighbors or others in close proximity to the premises where the animal is kept;
(5) Is in heat and not confined within a secure enclosure to prevent unintentional breeding; or
(6) Chases vehicles, bicycles, or people.
(b) Any such public nuisance animal may be impounded immediately by an animal control officer and the owner, possessor, guardian, or custodian may be charged for a violation of this section. The animal may remain impounded pending disposition of the charges by the court.
(Ord. No. 2007-10, § 10-32)
